 "prompt": "The repository at /testbed has a bug, described below. Fix it by changing the source code; do not modify or\nadd tests. When you are done, the project's existing test suite should pass.\n\n<issue>\n# ZipFinder breaks when entry_path is already in sys.path_importer_cache\n\nWhen using astroid to analyze code that imports modules from zip files, the ZipFinder class has a bug that prevents it from properly finding modules in zip files that are already in the sys.path_importer_cache.\n\n## Description\n\nThe current implementation of ZipFinder has a logic error in its initialization. When an entry path is already in the sys.path_importer_cache, it tries to remove and re-add it, but the error handling is incorrect, causing it to break out of the loop entirely when an exception occurs.\n\nThis affects the ability to find modules in zip files and egg files, causing imports to fail unexpectedly.\n\n## Steps to reproduce\n\n```python\nimport sys\nimport zipimport\nfrom astroid.interpreter._import import spec\n\n# First add a zip file to the path importer cache\nzip_path = \"path/to/some/package.zip\"\nsys.path_importer_cache[zip_path] = zipimport.zipimporter(zip_path)\n\n# Now try to find a module in that zip file\n# This will fail because ZipFinder breaks when the path is already in the cache\nfound_spec = spec.find_spec([\"my_package\"], [zip_path])\nprint(found_spec)  # This will not find the module correctly\n```\n\nExpected behavior: The ZipFinder should properly handle paths that are already in sys.path_importer_cache and still be able to find modules in those zip files.\n\nActual behavior: The ZipFinder fails to find modules in zip files that are already in sys.path_importer_cache.\n\nThis issue also affects the ClassModel's mro() method, which is returning incorrect results due to related changes.\n</issue>\n",
